HISTORY OF PGP && GPG && SIGSTORE AND COSIGN 
Alright. Full timeline. Because this stuff is basically Linux security civilization lore.

---

# The Ancient Era — “How do we trust software?”

Back in the early internet days, people realized:

```text id="5fjlwm"
Downloading files from the internet is sketchy as hell.
```

Anybody could:

* modify files
* impersonate developers
* inject malware
* MITM downloads

So cryptography became the answer.

---

# PGP — The OG

In 1991, Phil Zimmermann created:

## PGP

Pretty Good Privacy

This was revolutionary.

Before PGP:

* encryption was mostly military/government stuff
* strong crypto was heavily restricted

PGP gave regular people:

* encryption
* signing
* identity verification

using:

* public keys
* private keys

---

# Core idea

You create TWO keys:

## Public key

You share this openly.

## Private key

You NEVER share this.

---

# Signing flow

You sign a file with your PRIVATE key:

```text id="ks4v6t"
release.tar.gz -> release.tar.gz.sig
```

Others verify using your PUBLIC key.

If verification succeeds:

* file unchanged
* file authentic

Boom.

Supply-chain security born.

---

# The “Web of Trust”

PGP had this very nerdy anarchist idea:

```text id="g70n1i"
No central authority.
Humans verify humans.
```

People literally met at conferences to:

* compare fingerprints
* verify IDs
* sign each other’s keys

Called:

## Key Signing Parties

Peak Linux wizard culture.

---

# Then came legal chaos

US government treated strong crypto almost like weapons exports back then.

PGP had licensing/patent drama.

So the GNU people said:

> “Fine. We’ll build our own.”

---

# GPG Arrives

GNU Privacy Guard.

Command:

```bash id="llhz7f"
gpg
```

This became:

* free
* open source
* Linux-native
* OpenPGP-compatible

This is what almost every distro/tooling ecosystem uses now.

---

# So what is GPG REALLY?

GPG is:

## a tool for:

* encryption
* decryption
* signing
* verification
* key management

using OpenPGP standards.

---

# Why GPG became huge

Because Linux distributions needed trust.

Imagine:

* millions of packages
* mirrors worldwide
* random servers
* no HTTPS everywhere yet

How do you know packages are real?

So package managers evolved around signatures.

Examples:

* `apt`
* `rpm`
* `pacman`
* `portage`

all heavily use signed metadata.

---

# Example real-world flow

## Debian/Ubuntu

When you run:

```bash id="y1n5sx"
apt update
```

APT checks:

* repository metadata
* GPG signatures
* trusted keys

before installing packages.

That’s why expired repo keys break updates.

You’ve probably seen:

```text id="4m1sv5"
NO_PUBKEY
```

That’s GPG infrastructure yelling:

> “I don't trust this repo.”

---

# The Problems with GPG

Now comes the pain.

GPG is respected.

But HOLY HELL the UX.

---

## Problem 1 — Key management

People lose:

* private keys
* passphrases
* backups

constantly.

---

## Problem 2 — Trust is confusing

```text id="1v7w6z"
ultimate trust
full trust
marginal trust
unknown trust
```

Junior engineers look at this like ancient rune magic.

---

## Problem 3 — Keyservers became cursed

Public keyservers became:

* polluted
* spammed
* unreliable

There were even key poisoning attacks.

---

## Problem 4 — CI/CD sucks with long-lived private keys

Modern infra pipelines hate:

* manually managed secrets
* static signing keys
* permanent credentials

Cloud-native philosophy became:

```text id="jlwmu4"
Ephemeral identity > permanent secrets
```

That changed everything.

---

# Modern Era — Sigstore

Then came:

[Sigstore](https://www.sigstore.dev?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

This is basically:

> “What if signing software DIDN’T suck?”

Created by people from:

* Kubernetes ecosystem
* Linux Foundation circles
* supply-chain security world

---

# What Sigstore changes

Instead of:

```text id="czlk6u"
Download my public key manually.
```

Sigstore says:

```text id="kz4z5o"
Use your identity provider.
```

Examples:

* GitHub Actions identity
* Google identity
* Microsoft identity
* OIDC tokens

---

# Enter Cosign

[Cosign](https://docs.sigstore.dev/cosign/overview/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Cosign is the actual signing tool.

Like:

* GPG = old signing tool
* Cosign = modern cloud-native signing tool

---

# Example Cosign flow

Container image:

```bash id="0rr5qz"
docker build -t myapp .
```

Sign it:

```bash id="mx14y0"
cosign sign myimage
```

Verification:

```bash id="o9wnxw"
cosign verify myimage
```

---

# Why infra engineers love Cosign

Because it works naturally with:

* Kubernetes
* OCI registries
* GitHub Actions
* ephemeral credentials
* OIDC federation
* keyless signing

---

# Keyless signing

THIS is the big innovation.

Old world:

```text id="55ntj4"
Generate private key.
Store private key forever.
Protect private key forever.
Rotate keys manually.
Cry eventually.
```

New world:

```text id="jq1h0d"
Temporary identity token signs artifact.
```

Much cleaner for automation.

---

# Rekor — transparency log

Sigstore also introduced:

## Rekor

A public tamper-evident transparency log.

Meaning:

* signatures become publicly auditable
* malicious signatures are detectable
* ecosystem trust improves

Inspired partly by Certificate Transparency systems.

---

# So what's used where?

| Ecosystem               | Common Tech                 |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Linux distros           | GPG                         |
| Git signing             | GPG / SSH / Sigstore        |
| OCI containers          | Cosign                      |
| Kubernetes security     | Cosign                      |
| Modern CI/CD provenance | Sigstore                    |
| Legacy package systems  | GPG                         |
| Release verification    | GPG + Sigstore increasingly |

---

# The philosophical difference

## GPG philosophy

```text id="qmd1of"
Humans manage trust manually.
```

Decentralized.
Very hacker culture.
Very cypherpunk.

---

## Sigstore philosophy

```text id="62iz4s"
Cloud identity systems manage trust automatically.
```

Automation-first.
CI/CD-first.
Enterprise-friendly.

---

# The current reality

GPG is NOT dead.

Far from it.

But Sigstore/Cosign is becoming the future for:

* supply-chain security
* SBOM attestation
* artifact provenance
* Kubernetes ecosystems

Especially after:

* SolarWinds
* xz backdoor panic
* dependency-chain attacks

The industry basically realized:

```text id="c1rlsi"
“We need verifiable software origins.”
```

That’s the modern movement you’re watching happen in real time.

